I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 9 of 943 (part 4 of 7).\par\par\par
The Envelope in the Silverware Drawer\par\par\par
There was $3,200 in an envelope in the drawer next to the forks, and by Thursday night at 11:47 pm there wasn't.\par\par\par
There was $3,200 in an envelope in the drawer next to the forks, and by Thursday night at 11:47 pm there wasn't.\par\par\par
My daughter was seventeen. Her top row was crooked in a way that made her stop smiling in photos around age fourteen. My wife had been saving for her braces for two years, in cash, because our checking account was a war zone I didn't fully understand yet and she'd learned, without ever quite admitting it to herself, that money in the account had a way of disappearing.\par\par\par
The envelope was white. It said BRACES on it in black Sharpie in my wife's handwriting. My wife has beautiful handwriting. It's one of the things I noticed first about her, in 2001, on a card she gave me for my birthday when we were dating. She wrote BRACES on this envelope like she was writing a wedding invitation.\par\par\par
I opened the drawer on a Thursday in October 2022 because I needed a bottle opener. I was making myself a beer. The Thursday night college game had just started \emdash  a MAC game, some school in Ohio against some school in Michigan, on ESPN 2. I had already put $400 on the underdog earlier that week and lost. I had already put $250 on a Wednesday MLB playoff game and lost. I was down about $1,100 for the week, which was not a catastrophic week by 2022 standards, but which was giving me the specific tight feeling in my chest that I had come to recognize as the moment before I did something stupid to fix it.\par\par\par
Mikey \emdash  the bar guy, the one with the fake cousin in Vegas \emdash  had texted me at 5:12 pm. The line on the Thursday MAC game had moved three points in the last hour. Sharp money, he said. Lock. He used the word 'mortgage,' which was our shorthand for a bet you'd bet your mortgage on. I didn't have a mortgage. I had an envelope.\par\par\par
I saw the envelope when I was looking for the bottle opener. I want to be honest about the next thirty seconds because they are the thirty seconds I've thought about more than any other thirty seconds of my life.\par\par\par
I saw it. I looked at it. I closed the drawer. I opened the drawer again. I picked it up. I felt the thickness of it. My wife had told me at some point, maybe two months earlier, that she had $3,200 in it. I did the math in my head, which is a thing gamblers do \emdash  you don't even need paper. If I put the whole thing on the MAC game at minus 110, I'd win $2,909. I could put back the $3,200 by tomorrow morning, take my losses for the week to zero, and have almost three grand left over as breathing room.\par\par\par
Breathing room. That's the phrase I remember thinking. Like the money in the envelope was suffocating me.\par\par\par
I took the envelope. I closed the drawer. I went into the garage. I sat on a folding chair I keep out there next to a workbench I've never used. I opened the app on my phone. I deposited $3,200 via instant bank transfer. It cleared in about ninety seconds. I put the whole thing on the underdog getting three-and-a-half points in the MAC game, which had already started, second quarter, tied at 7.\par\par\par
My team went up 14-7 at half. 21-7 in the third. 24-10 with eight minutes left. I stood up from the folding chair at 24-10 with eight minutes left and I actually \emdash  I want you to picture this \emdash  I actually walked back into the kitchen. I opened the drawer. I looked at the empty spot where the envelope had been. I said, out loud, to nobody: I'm going to put it back tomorrow.\par\par\par
The other team scored with 4:12 left. Then again with 1:38 left. Then a two-point conversion. Then an onside kick they somehow recovered. Then a 41-yard field goal as time expired.\par\par\par
Final: 27-24. The underdog lost by three. I needed three-and-a-half.\par\par\par
I sat in the garage for an hour after the game ended. Then I did the coward things. I'm going to tell you about those next week. All of them. Even the one I've never told my wife.\par\par\par
Next: eleven days of lies, one 3 am spreadsheet, and the specific coward's math of how you almost don't tell her.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
